        // To simulate the external send failing, we will call Collect from the actor whose receive will forward to the bad reverter when receiving funds.
        // Instead we make the recipient be the bad contract by having actor be bad when calling Collect: Use vm.prank(bad) to make msg.sender==bad.
        // First ensure the bad contract has some ETH so that the low-level call target (msg.sender) exists with code but may still cause revert on receive
        vm.deal(address(bad), 1 wei);

        // Before Collect, record stored balance via public getter Acc(actor).balance
        uint256 before = c.Acc(actor).balance;

        // Now attempt Collect as if bad contract is the caller; to withdraw from actor's account we must have msg.sender == actor in original design.
        // But Collect withdraws to msg.sender; we want the withdrawal to go to a failing callee. So call Collect from bad and target withdrawal amount withdrawAmt
        // For the call to succeed in reference, the caller's recorded balance must be >= withdrawAmt; ensure that by restricting withdrawAmt
        vm.assume(before >= withdrawAmt);

        bool ok;
        try c.Collect{value: 0}(withdrawAmt) {
            ok = true;
        } catch {
            ok = false;
        }

        // After call, read recorded balance for the caller (msg.sender). But since in our test the account whose balance was funded was `actor`, and the Collect call was made by this test contract
        // we must reflect the model: in order for c.Acc(...) to refer to the account that performed Put, actor must be msg.sender when calling Put. We did that.
